From 3b44d7ba8ddc1943fee41646aa45f66373e5978c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lucas Santos <lsantos7654@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 1 May 2026 00:04:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 22/25] Add module-level liveness probe for batched-event
 scenarios
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0021 hooked every disconnect event the abstraction layers
expose: `stream.onAbort`, `c.req.raw.signal`, `incoming.on('close')`,
`incoming.on('aborted')`, `outgoing.on('close')`. Tests passed; the
user's actual production scenario still leaks: open 2 TUIs on
different sessions, close 1, count stays at 2 until a third TUI
connects.

The bug is one layer below the events we're listening to. Bun's
Node http compat batches socket-close events when the process has
steady JS work and other live sockets — they sit in a queue and
are delivered only when other I/O wakes the loop. Opening a third
TUI is the trigger because its inbound connection is the I/O event
that pumps the loop. Tests don't reproduce this because the test
process is otherwise quiet.

Fix: a server-side liveness probe at module scope. One
`setInterval(..., 2_000).unref()`. Iterates `_clients`, checks each
client's Node `outgoing.destroyed | writableEnded | socket.destroyed`,
and self-evicts via the entry's `stop()` callback. Bypasses the
event queue by polling the underlying socket state directly.

Per-client cost is constant (Map iteration + 3 boolean checks); the
probe runs once per process regardless of client count. The timer
is `.unref()`d so it doesn't keep tests or short-lived processes
alive past their natural lifetime.

The fast-path event handlers from patch 0021 stay — when they DO
fire, cleanup is instant (sub-ms). The probe is the safety net for
when Bun batches.

Each `_clients` entry now carries `outgoing` and `stop` references
so the sweep can probe and call cleanup. The other code paths
(`liveSessions`, `expandedLiveSet`, focus updates) only read
`entry.focus`, so they're unaffected.

Verification:
  - `bun test test/server/event.{test,real-server.test}.ts` — 8/8.
  - Production reproducer (open 2 TUIs, close 1, wait 3s) — count
    drops to 1 without opening anything else.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts | 51 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 49 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts b/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts
index 18ef44a..740529c 100644
--- a/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts
+++ b/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts
@@ -43,7 +43,51 @@ export const LiveSessionsChanged = BusEvent.define(
 // Subscriptions without an `X-Client-ID` header (the legacy "subscribe
 // to all events" shape) do not contribute to liveness — the route is
 // backwards-compatible for non-TUI consumers.
-const _clients = new Map<string, { focus: string | null }>()
+//
+// Each entry carries a reference to the underlying Node response
+// (`outgoing`) and the handler's `stop()` callback so the module-level
+// liveness probe (see `_sweep`) can directly check socket state and
+// self-evict if the client died but the abstraction-layer disconnect
+// signals haven't fired yet.
+type ClientEntry = {
+  focus: string | null
+  outgoing?: { destroyed?: boolean; writableEnded?: boolean; socket?: { destroyed?: boolean } }
+  stop?: () => void
+}
+const _clients = new Map<string, ClientEntry>()
+
+// Hivemind: server-side liveness probe. Fires every 2 seconds, iterates
+// `_clients`, and self-evicts any whose underlying Node `outgoing` is
+// destroyed/ended — i.e. the TUI's TCP socket is gone but the
+// `outgoing.on("close")` event hasn't been delivered to JS yet.
+//
+// Why this is necessary: production runs on `@hono/node-server`'s Node
+// http compat on Bun. When a busy server has multiple sockets and steady
+// JS work, Bun's I/O loop batches socket-close events and only delivers
+// them when other I/O (e.g. a new incoming connection) wakes the loop.
+// The user's reproducer is exactly this: closing one of two TUIs leaves
+// the count stale until a third TUI connects. The event handlers on the
+// SSE handler (`stream.onAbort`, `c.req.raw.signal`,
+// `incoming.on("close")`, `outgoing.on("close")`) all sit downstream of
+// that batched event — when it doesn't fire, none of them do.
+//
+// The probe bypasses the queue: it polls socket state directly. The
+// timer is `.unref()`d so it doesn't keep tests alive past their
+// natural lifetime.
+//
+// One timer at module scope, not per-client. Cost is constant in client
+// count: 2s interval × <1µs per probe iteration. Cheap.
+const _sweep = setInterval(() => {
+  for (const [clientID, entry] of _clients) {
+    const out = entry.outgoing
+    if (!out) continue
+    if (out.destroyed || out.writableEnded || out.socket?.destroyed) {
+      log.info("sweep evicting dead client", { clientID })
+      entry.stop?.()
+    }
+  }
+}, 2_000)
+_sweep.unref?.()

 function _publishChange(): void {
   // Fire-and-forget the expansion so call-sites stay sync. Each call
@@ -217,7 +261,10 @@ export const EventRoutes = () =>
           // to reflect itself until another client triggers the next
           // change.
           if (clientID) {
-            _clients.set(clientID, { focus: null })
+            // Record `outgoing` and `stop` on the entry so the
+            // module-level sweep can probe and self-evict if Bun
+            // batches the close event behind unrelated I/O.
+            _clients.set(clientID, { focus: null, outgoing: outgoing as ClientEntry["outgoing"], stop })
             _publishChange()
           }

